<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Afghanistan</h1><p class="st-meta">1926 CE – 1973 CE · capital Kabul</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Afghan monarchy of Amanullah and Zahir Shah, which pursued modernisation and neutrality for nearly half a century.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amanullah-khan">Amanullah Khan</a>, who had won full independence from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> in 1919, took the title king (padishah) in 1926, turning the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/saqqawist-emirate">Emirate of Afghanistan</a> into a kingdom. He launched ambitious reforms, including a constitution, schools for girls and the unveiling of women, inspired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-kemal-ataturk">Atatürk</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amanullah-khan">Amanullah</a>'s reforms provoked a revolt that drove him out in 1929, and after the brief <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/saqqawist-emirate">Saqqawist Emirate</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-nadir-shah">Mohammad Nadir Shah</a> took the throne. After his assassination in 1933 his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-zahir-shah">Mohammad Zahir Shah</a> reigned for forty years, at first through his uncles and then his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-daoud-khan">Mohammad Daoud Khan</a> as prime minister. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/islamic-emirate-of-afghanistan-2021-present">Afghanistan</a> stayed neutral in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, received aid from both the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a>, and adopted a liberal constitution in 1964.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In July 1973, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-zahir-shah">Zahir Shah</a> was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-daoud-khan">Daoud Khan</a> seized power in a bloodless coup and abolished the monarchy, proclaiming the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-afghanistan-1973-1978">Republic of Afghanistan</a> (1973-1978).</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-afghanistan.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Afghanistan" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Orange Tuesday at en.wikipedia. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Afghanistan_(1931%E2%80%931973).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1926 CE – 1973 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kabul</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Amanullah Khan, Mohammad Nadir Shah, Mohammad Zahir Shah</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Pashto, Persian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Afghanistan</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Afghan Kingdom</dd></dl></aside></div>
